window.QA_BANK = [{"module": 6, "slides": [2], "q": "What is memory, and how did Endel Tulving describe episodic memory?", "a": "Memory includes retaining, retrieving and using information about stimuli, images, events, ideas and skills after the original information is no longer present. A past experience can influence current or future behavior without being visible in the environment. Endel Tulving described episodic memory as a kind of mental time travel, because remembering an event connects it back to the experienced past.", "who": "Endel Tulving", "score": 1.5, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [3], "q": "What are encoding, storage and retrieval, and why does a failure to remember not tell you which one went wrong?", "a": "Encoding converts information into a form that can be stored, storage keeps it, and retrieval brings it back out when it is needed. Memory is an active system that can organize and even alter information, so it is not a recording that is kept unchanged. Because a failure to use information could come from a problem at any of the three operations, forgetting on its own does not show which one failed. Forgetting a familiar name could mean it was never encoded well, was lost from storage, or cannot be retrieved right now.", "score": 4.5, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [4], "q": "How is the memory tree organized, and where do episodic and semantic memory sit in it?", "a": "The tree splits memory into sensory memory, short-term memory and long-term memory. Long-term memory then splits into declarative memory, which contains episodic events and semantic knowledge, and procedural memory, which concerns skills. Declarative memory therefore has two branches, episodic memory and semantic memory. So episodic and semantic memory are branches of declarative memory inside long-term memory. They are not extra short-term stores, and the classification sorts memories by function rather than claiming all memories last equally long or hold the same kind of content.", "score": 4.5, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [5], "q": "What are the three stores of Atkinson and Shiffrin’s (1968) modal model, and what does each hold and for how long?", "a": "Sensory memory briefly retains incoming stimulation for seconds or fractions of a second. Short-term memory holds about five to seven items for roughly fifteen to twenty seconds. Long-term memory retains large amounts of information for years or even decades. Rehearsal is the control process that keeps information available in short-term memory and supports its transfer toward long-term memory.", "note": "The slides give short-term memory as five to seven items for about fifteen to twenty seconds. Modern work puts working capacity nearer three to four chunks, which matches the three to four items the slides give for visual short-term memory.", "who": "Atkinson and Shiffrin (1968)", "score": 8.0, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [6, 7, 8, 9, 10], "q": "What happens at each stage from looking at a phone directory to dialing a number?", "a": "Looking at the directory floods sensory memory with many pieces of visual information at once, and it is only held there very briefly. Attention picks out the one phone number you care about, and that selection moves it into short-term memory, while the rest of the directory is not carried forward. Repeating the number, which is maintenance rehearsal, keeps it available long enough to dial. If it is rehearsed enough it can become represented in long-term memory, so you no longer need the directory in view. Later you can retrieve it from long-term memory back into short-term memory to dial it, which shows that the flow goes both ways and is not only a one-way trip toward long-term storage.", "score": 1.133, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [11, 12], "q": "What are an icon and an echo, and what does persistence of vision show about sensory memory?", "a": "An icon is the fleeting visual trace left by stimulation, and an echo is the brief continuation of auditory information after a sound ends. They differ in modality, vision versus hearing, and they are not the durable representations used to remember something much later. Persistence of vision is the brief retention of the perception of light, and it is why a sparkler seems to leave a trail and why film frames feel continuous. It shows that sensory memory holds a lot of information for a short interval, which fills the gaps between moments of input, but its large capacity does not mean long duration, because the information decays quickly.", "note": "Film continuity is usually attributed to apparent motion (the phi phenomenon) and flicker fusion, not persistence of vision. Persistence of vision does account for the sparkler trail, and the icon is a brief stored trace, which is different from visible persistence.", "score": 3.633, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [13, 14, 15], "q": "What did Sperling (1960) do, and how do whole report, partial report and delayed partial report differ?", "a": "Sperling (1960) flashed an array of letters, three rows of four such as X M L T, A F N B, C D Z P, and asked what people had seen. The puzzle was whether people reported few letters because they only registered a few, or because the image faded before they could report everything. In whole report, participants report as many letters as they can. In partial report, a tone after the display tells them which row to report, so they cannot know in advance which row to memorize. In delayed partial report, the tone comes after an extra delay. The tone only comes after the display is gone, so strong partial report means information about all the rows was available at the start.", "who": "Sperling (1960)", "score": 5.322, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [16, 17, 18], "q": "What were Sperling’s (1960) results, and what do they show about the capacity and duration of sensory memory?", "a": "In Sperling (1960), whole report averaged 4.5 of 12 letters, about 37.5 percent. Immediate partial report averaged 3.3 of 4 letters, about 82 percent, no matter which row was cued. Delayed partial report fell to about 1 of 4, or 25 percent, in the delayed-cue condition. Because people could report almost any cued row, far more than four or five letters must have been available at first, so the whole-report limit is a limit on reporting before the trace fades, not on what was perceived. Sensory memory therefore has a large capacity but fast decay, with most information lost within about one second unless it is selected for further processing. As the cue is delayed, partial-report accuracy drops toward the whole-report level, which links the advantage to a rapidly fading representation and does not support holding the whole array indefinitely.", "who": "Sperling (1960)", "score": 7.767, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [19], "q": "What is memory span, and what is the traditional estimate for short-term memory?", "a": "Short-term memory holds small amounts briefly and is easily disrupted by interruption and interference. Memory span is the amount that can be recalled accurately in a task. The traditional estimate is seven plus or minus two units, but it depends on the kind of units and the task and should not be used for every visual-memory result.", "note": "Miller’s seven plus or minus two is about chunks, and modern estimates are nearer four chunks. The slides also give five to seven items elsewhere, so the two figures on the slides do not match each other.", "score": 0.5, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [20], "q": "How do recoding, maintenance rehearsal and elaborative rehearsal differ?", "a": "Recoding reorganizes or modifies information in short-term memory. Maintenance rehearsal repeats information to prolong its availability, like repeating a label. Elaborative rehearsal links new information with existing long-term knowledge, which supports longer-term learning. They are different control processes, because repeating a label is not the same as explaining how it relates to what you already know.", "score": 4.5, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [21, 22, 23], "q": "What does short-term memory do in current thought, what does Clive Wearing show, and how do duration and capacity differ?", "a": "Short-term memory includes selected sensory input and information retrieved from long-term memory, and it is what lets you understand a sentence, do arithmetic, dial a number or navigate. It acts as a window on the present, and this active availability differs from the large body of knowledge stored outside current awareness. Clive Wearing is a case of memory impairment. Keeping immediate information and remembering across events are different memory functions, so a case like his has to be analyzed function by function. Duration is how long information stays available and capacity is how much can be held. Rehearsal can extend duration and chunking can extend effective capacity, so a result can reflect the strategy used and not only the memory system.", "score": 3.345, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [24], "q": "Why did Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959) have participants count backward by threes?", "a": "Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959) presented three letters followed by a number, and participants counted backward by threes during a delay of 3 to 18 seconds and then recalled the letters. The counting prevents ordinary maintenance rehearsal, so participants cannot simply keep repeating the letters. Comparing the delays therefore measures retention when rehearsal is blocked.", "who": "Brown (1958); Peterson and Peterson (1959)", "score": 9.5, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [25, 26, 27], "q": "What happened to recall after a long delay on the first trial versus many trials later in Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959)?", "a": "In Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), on the first trial recall was high at three seconds and still relatively high at eighteen seconds, because no earlier letters from the task competed with the current set. By the third trial recall at eighteen seconds was lower than on the first trial, since earlier sets now competed. After many trials recall at long delays was substantially reduced, because accumulated earlier material interfered with the set being tested. The same delay did far more damage when more earlier material had piled up, so trial history matters and the drop is not only the passage of eighteen seconds.", "note": "Brown and Peterson and Peterson found the decline in recall with delay. The trial-by-trial proactive-interference analysis is usually credited to Keppel and Underwood (1962), who are not named on the slides.", "who": "Brown (1958); Peterson and Peterson (1959)", "score": 4.034, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [28, 29, 30], "q": "Why does the average over all trials give a misleading impression in Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959)?", "a": "In Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), the average over all trials makes it look like a steady loss with time, as though short-term memory simply decays. But the average mixes very different trials together. Early trials have high recall even at 18 seconds, and later trials have very low recall at 18 seconds. The steep drop comes mostly from the later trials, where proactive interference is strongest, so averaging hides interference behind something that looks like decay. Decay is loss associated with the passage of time, and proactive interference is when earlier information disrupts remembering newer information. Because earlier trials affect later recall, the Brown–Peterson pattern is evidence for interference and challenges treating every loss as pure time-based decay. The effective duration is about 15 to 20 seconds, and rehearsal and interference conditions affect the outcome.", "note": "The slides say recall at 18 seconds is relatively high on the first trial and substantially reduced on later trials, and that the average is a decline from shorter to longer delays that combines early trials with little interference and later trials with more. The words steady and very low are stronger than the slides.", "who": "Brown (1958); Peterson and Peterson (1959)", "score": 6.856, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [30], "q": "Which concept describes earlier trigrams making new letters harder to recall in Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959)?", "a": "It is proactive interference, which occurs when earlier information disrupts remembering newer information. In Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), earlier trials affect later recall, because the trigrams from earlier trials compete with the one being tested, so the loss is not explained solely by elapsed time.", "who": "Brown (1958); Peterson and Peterson (1959)", "score": 7.5, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [31, 32, 33], "q": "How does retroactive interference differ from proactive interference, and what is an example of each?", "a": "The direction of the competition is what differs. Retroactive interference is when new information disrupts memory for older information, so the affected memory is the old one and the interfering information arrived later. Learning a new phone number making the old number harder to retrieve is the example. Proactive interference is when old information disrupts memory for newer information, such as previous parking locations interfering with remembering where you parked today. Routine events are especially vulnerable because similar memories compete, and both types can be reduced when events are distinctive. To label it correctly, first decide which information is being recalled, then ask whether the competitor came earlier or later.", "score": 3.811, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [34, 35], "q": "What is digit span, and how is it scored?", "a": "Digit span is the longest sequence of digits recalled correctly in order. The lists get longer, from four items to ten, and the longest list reproduced completely and in order defines the span. Getting the digits right but out of order does not count, one successful short list does not establish the limit, and one missed digit makes a longer list incorrect. Capacity differs between individuals and can be affected by strategies.", "score": 3.367, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [36, 37], "q": "What did Miller (1956) propose, and how do Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004) change the picture?", "a": "Miller (1956), in The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two, proposed that short-term capacity is about seven units, where the unit can differ across tasks, such as digits, words, numbers or phrases. The title is not proof that every short-term system holds exactly seven raw sensory elements. A unit can hold different amounts of information, so Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004) compared colored squares with more complex objects using change detection. Their alternative emphasis is on the amount or complexity of information, not a fixed number of equally sized items.", "who": "Miller (1956); Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004)", "score": 8.5, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [37, 38, 39, 40, 41, 42], "q": "How does the change-detection task of Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004) work, and what did it show about visual short-term memory capacity?", "a": "Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004) used change detection to measure visual short-term memory, VSTM, by comparing displays after a delay. Participants briefly view a first array, S1, for 100 milliseconds, and it then disappears, so they must keep the relevant visual properties in memory. A second display, S2, appears for 2,000 milliseconds and they judge whether it is the same or different. Correct answers require comparing S2 with the remembered S1, not looking at two arrays at once. Accuracy falls sharply when the set size goes above roughly four items, giving a capacity of about three to four items in visual working memory. That differs from the seven-plus-or-minus-two estimate for other tasks, and different tasks do not have to measure one identical capacity.", "who": "Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004)", "score": 7.433, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [43, 44], "q": "How does stimulus complexity affect visual capacity in Alvarez and Cavanagh’s (2004) work?", "a": "In Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004), same–different performance depends on how complex the stimuli are. Colored squares support a higher estimated capacity than more complex polygons or shaded cubes, and Chinese-character-like forms were also compared. Treating every stimulus as an equally sized item hides these differences, because capacity is constrained by the amount of information needed to represent the objects. Familiarity and the structure given to a stimulus can also change what counts as a unit of information. So a capacity for one category of stimulus is not a universal estimate for all visual objects.", "who": "Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004)", "score": 6.367, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [45], "q": "What is chunking, and what did Ericsson and colleagues (1980) show?", "a": "Chunking combines smaller units into larger meaningful units. Ericsson and colleagues (1980) trained a student to reach a digit span of 79 by using familiar running times to organize the digits. The gain depends on knowledge-based organization, not on expanding every raw memory limit equally.", "who": "Ericsson and colleagues (1980)", "score": 9.667, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 6, "slides": [45, 46, 47], "q": "What did Chase and Simon (1973) find about chess recall, and what do control processes lead to?", "a": "In Chase and Simon (1973), chess masters recall more correct piece placements than beginners for meaningful game positions, but the advantage is greatly reduced for random arrangements. The experts’ edge comes from chunks that represent familiar relationships among pieces, and randomizing those relationships removes most of the benefit even though the pieces are the same kinds. Control processes include rehearsal, strategies for making material memorable such as the memory palace, and directing attention, and considering these active operations leads toward working memory, which concerns using and managing currently available information.", "who": "Chase and Simon (1973)", "score": 8.4, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 9, "slides": [2, 3], "q": "What is working memory according to Baddeley and Hitch (1974), and why does mental multiplication of 43 × 6 need both storage and processing?", "a": "Baddeley and Hitch (1974) proposed working memory as a limited-capacity system for the temporary storage and manipulation of information. Storage keeps information available for immediate use, and manipulation changes or works with it while it stays available. It is needed because treating short-term memory as passive storage is too simple, since comprehension, learning and reasoning require holding and operating on information together. In 43 × 6, first 3 × 6 = 18, the 8 is held as the ones digit (storage) and the 1 is carried over the 4 (an operation). Then 6 × 4 = 24, plus the carried 1 gives 25, and combining it with the held 8 gives 258. The 8 has to survive while another multiplication and an addition are carried out, which is exactly keeping a result available while changing other parts of the problem.", "who": "Baddeley and Hitch (1974)", "score": 6.6, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 9, "slides": [2, 4], "q": "How does Baddeley and Hitch’s (1974) working-memory model differ from Atkinson and Shiffrin’s (1968) short-term memory?", "a": "In Atkinson and Shiffrin’s (1968) model, a single short-term component holds different types of information for a short time, and the emphasis is on temporary availability. Baddeley and Hitch’s (1974) model separates the system into the central executive, the visuospatial sketchpad and the phonological loop, so visual and spatial information can be maintained in a different component from verbal and auditory information, with the central executive coordinating their use. Working memory is used for manipulating information during complex cognition. The episodic buffer is a fourth component added by Baddeley in 2000, so it belongs to the expanded model and not the original three-component proposal.", "who": "Baddeley and Hitch (1974); Atkinson and Shiffrin (1968)", "score": 7.181, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 9, "slides": [5, 6], "q": "What are the phonological loop, the visuospatial sketchpad and the central executive, and how do they connect to long-term knowledge?", "a": "The phonological loop maintains verbal and auditory information, with a phonological store that holds it and an articulatory rehearsal process that keeps it active by repeating the sounds, even silently. The visuospatial sketchpad holds visual information, what something looks like, and spatial information, where its parts are and how they are arranged. The central executive pulls information from long-term memory, coordinates the other components, and directs and maintains attention, and it is not a store for a particular kind of information. All the components interact with long-term knowledge, so a calculation can use verbal rehearsal of a result together with a visual picture of the digits.", "score": 4.0, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 9, "slides": [7], "q": "What are the phonological similarity effect, the word-length effect and articulatory suppression, and what does each one tell you about the phonological loop?", "a": "The phonological similarity effect is confusion between letters or words that sound alike, even when they look different. The word-length effect is that memory for lists is better for short words than long words, because longer words take more time to rehearse and produce. Articulatory suppression is speaking while trying to remember other material, which occupies the rehearsal process and so reduces memory. Similarity is about confusion between sound patterns, word length is about the time needed to repeat the items, and suppression is about interfering with rehearsal itself. Together they show that verbal maintenance depends on sound-based codes and speech-related rehearsal.", "score": 1.364, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 9, "slides": [8, 9], "q": "What did Conrad (1964) find, and why does it show that visually presented letters are remembered by sound?", "a": "Conrad (1964) briefly flashed letters, half the time similar and half not, and asked participants to write them down. Errors were more likely to swap in a letter that sounded like the target than one that only looked like it. An E was likely to be replaced by a B, which sounds similar, while a visually similar substitution such as F for E was less likely. The letters came in through vision, but the confusions followed their spoken names. So remembering a seen letter can involve converting it into a sound-based representation through the phonological loop. The key distinction is between the presentation format, what people see, and the memory code, the form in which it is maintained.", "who": "Conrad (1964)", "score": 8.6, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 9, "slides": [10], "q": "What did Baddeley and colleagues (1975, 1984) find about word length?", "a": "Baddeley and colleagues (1975, 1984) found that people remembered more short words than long words. The word-length effect concerns how long the items take to pronounce and rehearse, so a list of longer words takes more time to repeat and fewer words can be refreshed within the same brief rehearsal period.", "who": "Baddeley and colleagues (1975, 1984)", "score": 9.455, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 9, "slides": [10], "q": "What did Ellis and Hennelly (1980) find about digit span in Welsh and English?", "a": "Ellis and Hennelly (1980) found that Welsh–English bilingual children had a shorter digit span in Welsh than in English. The children knew both languages, but Welsh number names take longer to pronounce, so the difference follows the time needed to say the number names and not the numbers themselves. Span is related to the amount of verbal material that can be said in about 1.5 to 2 seconds, so faster rehearsal lets more items be kept active.", "who": "Ellis and Hennelly (1980)", "score": 9.455, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 9, "slides": [11], "q": "What happens to memory, the word-length effect and the phonological similarity effect under articulatory suppression, and why?", "a": "When a person speaks while memorizing, the competing speech keeps the phonological loop busy, so there is less opportunity to rehearse and recall gets worse. The word-length effect is reduced, because short words normally win by being quicker to rehearse, and when rehearsal is occupied neither list gets ordinary rehearsal. The phonological similarity effect is also reduced, because the conversion of seen items into sounds is disrupted, so sound overlap has less influence. This connects the memory effects to a particular process, speech-related rehearsal used to maintain verbal information.", "note": "The phonological similarity effect is abolished by articulatory suppression only when items are presented visually. With auditory presentation it persists.", "score": 6.364, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 9, "slides": [12, 13], "q": "What did Brooks (1968) find in the sentence task?", "a": "The visuospatial sketchpad holds visual and spatial information, such as the shape of an object and where its parts are. In Brooks (1968), participants memorized a sentence and decided for each word whether it was a noun, responding either by speaking or by pointing. Pointing was easier, because maintaining and processing the sentence occupied the phonological loop, and speaking the answers also required verbal processing and competed with it.", "who": "Brooks (1968)", "score": 8.182, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 9, "slides": [14, 15], "q": "What did Brooks (1968) find in the F task, and what does the opposite pattern show?", "a": "In Brooks (1968), participants memorized the shape of an F, mentally followed its outline and classified each corner as an inside or an outside corner, responding either by speaking or by pointing to response locations. Speaking was easier, because holding and following the F used the visuospatial sketchpad, and pointing to spatially arranged responses added a competing spatial demand. This is the opposite of the sentence-task result, and it shows that verbal and visual–spatial memory are at least partly independent. A task is easier when the information held and the operation performed use different types of short-term memory.", "who": "Brooks (1968)", "score": 9.473, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 9, "slides": [17], "q": "What did Shepard and Metzler (1971) find about mental rotation, and what does the pattern of reaction times mean?", "a": "Shepard and Metzler (1971) presented pairs of block-built objects at different orientations, and participants judged whether the objects matched. Mental rotation means turning a representation in mind to compare how its parts line up, and the rotation can be in the picture plane or in depth. For picture-plane pairs, reaction time increased as the angle of rotation increased, approximately linearly, so small angles were answered faster than large ones. A larger angle requires a larger mental transformation before the objects can be compared. This suggests an active operation on a spatial representation, not just storage of an unchanged picture.", "who": "Shepard and Metzler (1971)", "score": 9.545, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 9, "slides": [18], "q": "What does the central executive do, what are updating, shifting and inhibition, and why is the executive called a homunculus?", "a": "The central executive does the work of working memory by directing and maintaining attention, which includes focusing on relevant information, dividing attention across demands and switching attention when the task changes. It also suppresses irrelevant information, coordinates the sketchpad and the phonological loop, and performs calculations. Miyake and colleagues (2000) distinguish updating, which changes the information currently maintained, shifting, which changes the task or operation being performed, and inhibition, which suppresses information or responses that interfere with the goal. The executive is criticized as a homunculus, a little internal agent given the job of controlling everything, because naming a controller does not explain how control is done. Splitting it into updating, shifting and inhibition specifies different operations inside what would otherwise be one broad label.", "who": "Miyake and colleagues (2000)", "score": 6.636, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 9, "slides": [19], "q": "What is the episodic buffer, and what evidence supports a component that goes beyond the loop and the sketchpad?", "a": "Baddeley (2000) added the episodic buffer as a fourth component. It has a limited capacity of about four chunks, and it binds information from the phonological loop, the visuospatial sketchpad and long-term memory into integrated episodes. Binding means keeping different kinds of information together as a connected representation. People can immediately recall about 16 words from a meaningful sentence, more than their span for unrelated words, because meaning and long-term language knowledge let the words be kept as connected material. Some amnesic patients can immediately recall a just-heard story, so immediate retention of an organized episode can be present even when longer-term memory is impaired.", "who": "Baddeley (2000)", "score": 6.545, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 9, "slides": [20], "q": "What did Vogel and colleagues (2005) do in their working-memory task?", "a": "Vogel and colleagues (2005) first measured working-memory capacity to separate higher- and lower-capacity participants. Participants then did a visual memory task while event-related potentials (ERPs), electrical brain responses, were recorded. A cue pointed to one side of the display, red bars were shown for 100 milliseconds, there was a 900 millisecond retention interval, and then a test display where they judged whether a relevant orientation had changed. The more complex displays also had irrelevant blue bars, so ignoring them required selection within the display.", "who": "Vogel and colleagues (2005)", "score": 6.727, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 9, "slides": [21, 22], "q": "What did Vogel and colleagues (2005) find about capacity and filtering efficiency?", "a": "In Vogel and colleagues (2005), the memory-related response continues into the retention interval, and for high-capacity participants it is greater with four relevant items than with two. Adding two blue distractors to two red targets changed little for high-capacity participants, so they kept the irrelevant items out. For low-capacity participants, the distractors raised the response toward the four-item level, meaning irrelevant material entered working memory. Capacity was positively correlated with filtering efficiency, so people with higher capacity are also less distracted, and a lower score can reflect competing material occupying processing that targets needed.", "who": "Vogel and colleagues (2005)", "score": 7.709, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 10, "slides": [2], "q": "What makes something a problem, and why does knowing the goal not mean the problem is solved?", "a": "A problem has a present state, a goal and an obstacle between them, and the solution is not immediately obvious. The present state is the situation as it is, the goal is the situation you are trying to reach, and the obstacle blocks an immediate move from one to the other. In the numbered-tile puzzle you can state exactly what the finished arrangement looks like and still have a problem, because the sequence of moves that gets there is unclear. The difficulty is the route, not whether the goal has been stated.", "score": 1.375, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 10, "slides": [3], "q": "What is the Gestalt approach to problem solving (Koffka, 1935), and what is restructuring in Köhler’s circle problem?", "a": "The Gestalt approach takes a perceptual view of problem solving, associated with Koffka (1935). A problem is represented in the mind, so how a person organizes it affects which solution becomes available. Restructuring means reorganizing the representation so a hidden relationship becomes usable. In Köhler’s circle problem you must find the length of line x when the radius is r. The black line is one diagonal of a rectangle whose lower-right corner is at the circle’s center, and the dashed line from the upper-left corner on the circle to the center is a radius. The two diagonals of a rectangle are equal, so x = r.", "who": "Koffka (1935)", "score": 9.625, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 10, "slides": [4], "q": "What is insight, and does every problem get solved with an Aha experience?", "a": "Insight is the sudden realization of a problem’s solution. A person first perceives an object or problem one way and then represents it differently, and discovering a crucial element in the changed representation makes the solution available all at once. Gestalt psychologists connected this with the Aha experience, a sudden change from not having the solution to recognizing it. Not every problem is solved this way, because some allow a more methodical process in which successive operations bring the person closer to an answer.", "score": 4.25, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 10, "slides": [5, 7], "q": "What did Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987) do and find with insight and algebra problems?", "a": "Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987) compared insight problems, the triangle and chain problems, with noninsight problems such as algebra. Every 15 seconds participants gave warmth ratings of how close they felt to a solution, with hot meaning close and colder meaning far. The prediction was that for insight problems people would not be good at anticipating how near they were, because a change in representation can make the answer appear without growing feelings of progress. For algebra, warmth ratings rose gradually as the solution approached. For insight problems, warmth stayed low until close to the solution and then jumped sharply, from about 3 to 7.", "who": "Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)", "score": 9.475, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 10, "slides": [5, 6], "q": "How are the triangle problem and the chain problem solved in Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)?", "a": "In Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987), the triangle problem has ten dots that form an upward-pointing triangle and you must move exactly three dots to make it point down, with seven staying in place. The original top dot becomes a new bottom point beneath the center of the original bottom row, and the two outer dots of the original bottom row move to the ends of the two-dot row, giving rows of four, three, two and one. In the chain problem, four short sections of chain must become one closed loop. Opening a link costs 2 cents, closing it costs 3 cents, and the budget is 15 cents, so three open-and-close operations are possible. One three-link section is opened into three separate links, and those links connect the other three sections into a closed loop, using the full 15 cents.", "who": "Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)", "score": 8.55, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 10, "slides": [8], "q": "What are fixation and functional fixedness, and what happens in the candle problem?", "a": "Fixation is a tendency to focus on one characteristic of a problem in a way that blocks a solution, and Gestalt psychologists saw it as a major obstacle. Functional fixedness restricts an object to its familiar function. In the candle problem you have a candle, matches and their box, and tacks, and must mount the candle on a corkboard so wax does not drip on the floor. The solution is to tack the matchbox to the board and use it as a platform. Seeing the box only as a container for matches blocks that, and participants given empty matchboxes were twice as likely to solve it.", "score": 6.5, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 10, "slides": [9, 10], "q": "How do the two-string problem and the swinging-string hint show functional fixedness and restructuring?", "a": "In the two-string problem you must tie together two strings hanging too far apart to reach both at once. A pair of pliers can be tied to one string as a weight so it swings like a pendulum, and you catch it while holding the other string. The familiar grip function of the pliers blocks seeing the weight. When an experimenter accidentally set a string in motion, most participants solved the problem, because the hint restructured the representation, so the string became something that could be made to swing within reach.", "note": "In the original study the experimenter set the string moving deliberately, not accidentally, as the slide words it.", "score": 6.475, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 10, "slides": [10, 11], "q": "What is a mental set, and how do the water-jug problems show it?", "a": "A mental set is a preconceived notion about how to approach a problem, based on past experience with that problem or similar ones. It concerns the method of solving, so a person keeps applying a familiar procedure even when another approach would work. In the water-jug problems you get three empty jugs with given capacities and a target amount. In the first problem A holds 21, B holds 127, C holds 3 and the goal is 100, and the solution is B − A − 2C. Repeated success with that procedure makes it the expected method. In problem 7 (A 15, B 39, C 3, goal 18) the familiar method still works, but A + C gives 18 more directly. In problem 8 (A 28, B 59, C 3, goal 25) A − C gives 25 directly. The mental set inhibits the simpler solution even though the familiar one remains possible.", "score": 6.475, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 10, "slides": [12], "q": "What did Newell and Simon (1956) mean by problem solving as search through a problem space?", "a": "Newell and Simon (1956) developed the Logic Theorist and described problem solving as a search that happens between posing a problem and reaching its solution. A problem space includes the initial state, the intermediate states along the way and the goal state. Knowing the initial and goal states does not tell you which intermediate states connect them, so solving means finding a sequence that reaches the goal.", "who": "Newell and Simon (1956)", "score": 7.875, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 10, "slides": [13, 14], "q": "What are operators, and how do they work in the Tower of Hanoi?", "a": "Operators are the rules that say which moves are allowed, and applying one changes the problem from one state to another. In the Tower of Hanoi you move one disk at a time, you can only move a disk with nothing on top of it, and a larger disk can never be placed on a smaller one, even in intermediate arrangements. The starting stack is the initial state, the completed stack on the target peg is the goal state, and temporary distributions across the pegs are intermediate states. Smaller disks have to be moved out of the way repeatedly, and the 64-disk version would take roughly a trillion years at one move per second.", "note": "The minimum for 64 disks is 2^64 − 1 moves, which at one move per second is about 585 billion years, so a trillion years is a rounded overstatement.", "score": 6.15, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 10, "slides": [15, 16], "q": "What are the initial, intermediate and goal states in the numbered-tile puzzle, and what is a search tree?", "a": "The puzzle has eight tiles and one empty space on a three-by-three board. The initial state is 8–empty–6, 5–4–7, 2–3–1, and the goal state is empty–1–2, 3–4–5, 6–7–8. A tile being on the board does not mean it is in its required position, so the whole arrangement defines the state. A search tree shows the possible states and the moves connecting them. From a start such as 2–8–3, 1–6–4, 7–empty–5, the empty space can shift left, up or right by swapping with an adjacent tile, and each result is a different intermediate state with the same eight tiles. Each state can have several successors, so the solver chooses which branches to pursue, and a connected sequence of states supplies a route to the goal.", "score": 5.775, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 10, "slides": [17, 18], "q": "What is means–end analysis, and what are the subgoals in the Tower of Hanoi?", "a": "Newell and Simon’s approach to solving as search leads to means–end analysis, which organizes the search around reducing the difference between the current situation and the goal. A subgoal is a condition that makes part of the larger task possible, not the final answer. In the Tower of Hanoi the large disk cannot move while smaller disks sit on it, so the first subgoal is freeing the large disk. The smallest disk goes to the third peg and the next disk to the middle peg. The third peg must then be free for the large disk, so the second subgoal is freeing the third peg, by moving the smallest disk from the third peg onto the smaller stack on the middle peg. The third subgoal is moving the large disk from the starting peg to the third peg, and the smaller disks still have to be brought over afterward. Different subgoals address different obstacles to the same move, one at the source and one at the destination.", "note": "The slides give no year for means–end analysis. The 1956 date belongs to the Logic Theorist, and means–end analysis is usually dated to Newell and Simon’s later General Problem Solver work.", "score": 6.6, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 10, "slides": [19, 20], "q": "How does the mutilated-checkerboard problem work, and why is covering it impossible?", "a": "An ordinary checkerboard has 64 squares and 32 dominoes can cover it, with each domino covering two adjacent squares of different colors. In the mutilated version two opposite corners are removed, leaving 62 squares, which matches the area of 31 dominoes. But the two removed corners are the same color, so the board has fewer squares of that color, and every domino covers one square of each color. The covering would need equal numbers of the two colors, so it is impossible. Equal total area does not guarantee a legal arrangement, and the color imbalance applies to every possible placement.", "score": 6.225, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 10, "slides": [21], "q": "What did the bread-and-butter condition show about how a problem is stated?", "a": "How a problem is stated changes its difficulty. Participants got a blank board, a colored board, a board labeled black and pink, or one labeled bread and butter. The bread-and-butter labels emphasized the difference between the square types and their pairing, which made it easier to represent a domino as needing one of each kind. Participants in that condition solved the problem twice as fast as those in the blank condition.", "score": 7.0, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 10, "slides": [22], "q": "What is a think-aloud protocol, and what does it reveal in the bread-and-butter checkerboard problem?", "a": "A think-aloud protocol asks a person to say aloud what they are thinking while solving, verbalizing new thoughts and not just describing their actions. In the bread-and-butter version, participants can reach the solution once they recognize that the words bread and butter matter for the required pairing. Their thinking shifts from treating squares as positions to considering the relationship between the two kinds of square. That is a representational shift related to the Gestalt account of restructuring, since the objects and the covering requirement stay the same but the solver uses a different relationship among them. Verbal reports can show when the pairing enters the person’s reasoning.", "score": 6.625, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 11, "slides": [2], "q": "How do representation and insight, search, and problem statement each affect problem solving?", "a": "Reorganization and insight concern how a problem is represented, since a person may organize the elements so that a useful relationship is hidden, and reorganizing can make the solution available, sometimes suddenly. Problem solving as search concerns the activity between posing a problem and reaching its solution, finding a route through possible states, and naming the start and goal does not give the route. How a problem is stated affects difficulty because the presentation influences which relationships a person represents. In the mutilated checkerboard, you can think in terms of total area or in terms of pairing different-colored squares, and the color relationship exposes a constraint that area alone does not.", "score": 1.333, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 11, "slides": [3, 4], "q": "What is the Russian marriage problem, why is the answer no, and how does it match the mutilated checkerboard?", "a": "There are 32 bachelors and 32 unmarried women, and a matchmaker arranges 32 heterosexual marriages. Two bachelors die after a contest involving pierogies, leaving 62 survivors, 30 men and 32 women. The question is whether 31 heterosexual marriages can be formed. The answer is no, because each marriage needs one member of each group and the groups are unequal, so at most 30 marriages can form and two women are left without a partner. Dividing 62 people by two gives 31 pairs by total size, but that ignores which kinds of person each pair must contain, and no rearrangement creates another man. In the checkerboard, a domino covers two adjacent squares, one of each color, and removing two opposite corners removes two squares of the same color, so the colors are unequal even though 62 squares matches the area of 31 dominoes. Moving the dominoes around cannot change that imbalance.", "score": 3.489, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 11, "slides": [5], "q": "What is analogical problem solving, and how does the Russian marriage problem serve as a source for the checkerboard?", "a": "Analogical problem solving uses the solution to a similar problem to guide the solution to a new one. The target problem is the one currently being solved, and a source problem shares relevant similarities with it. The Russian marriage problem is the source and the mutilated checkerboard is the target. Marriages correspond to dominoes, the two groups of people correspond to the two square colors, and a valid marriage needs one member of each group, just as a domino needs one square of each color. Removing two bachelors corresponds to removing two same-colored squares, since both leave an imbalance. Analogical transfer is the transfer from source to target, and the useful information is the relationship among the elements, not their identical appearance.", "score": 6.667, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 11, "slides": [6], "q": "What is Duncker’s radiation problem, and how is it solved?", "a": "Duncker’s radiation problem describes a patient who has a malignant tumor in the stomach that cannot be operated on, and a ray can destroy the tumor only if high enough intensity reaches it at once. A high-intensity ray would also destroy the healthy tissue it passes through, and a low-intensity ray would spare the tissue but not destroy the tumor. The solution is several low-intensity rays converging on the tumor from different directions at the same time, so each path through the tissue gets low intensity while the combined intensity at the tumor is enough. Only about 10 percent of participants reach this without the helpful analogy.", "score": 7.556, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 11, "slides": [7, 8], "q": "How does the fortress problem correspond to Duncker’s radiation problem?", "a": "A rebel general has an army strong enough to take a dictator’s fortress, but mines on each road explode if a large force passes. Small groups can travel safely along different roads and arrive together, and combined they are strong enough to attack. The fortress corresponds to the tumor in Duncker’s radiation problem, the small groups to the low-intensity rays, and the roads to the paths through tissue. Sending the whole army down one road matches sending one high-intensity ray through healthy tissue. The groups have to arrive together, because merely dividing the army or lowering every ray’s intensity without combining the effects leaves the target requirement unmet.", "score": 6.0, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 11, "slides": [9, 10], "q": "What are noticing, mapping and applying in Gick and Holyoak’s analogy steps, and what happened when participants were reminded of the story?", "a": "Gick and Holyoak distinguished noticing that a source may be relevant to the target, mapping the correspondences between their elements and relationships, and applying the mapping to generate a parallel solution. Noticing is crucial and can be the hardest step. After reading the fortress story, only about 30 percent of participants solved the radiation problem, so having the story in memory did not guarantee they used it spontaneously. When they were told to think about the story they had read, success rose to about 75 percent. The reminder made the relevant source available for mapping and application. Surface features, such as a fortress, an army, a tumor or a ray, can hide a shared relationship, since a fortress and a tumor do not look alike, and making surface features more similar can make the relationship easier to notice.", "who": "Gick and Holyoak", "score": 7.622, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 11, "slides": [11, 12], "q": "What is the lightbulb problem, and how well does Duncker’s radiation problem work as a source for it?", "a": "Ruth finds an expensive laboratory lightbulb with a broken filament, and the surrounding glass is sealed, so she cannot open it. A brief, high-intensity laser would fuse the filament but also break the fragile glass, and a low-intensity beam would save the glass but not fuse the filament. The solution is several low-intensity lasers directed at the filament from different directions at once, so their combined effect fuses it while each part of the glass gets only a low-intensity beam. Duncker’s radiation problem acts as a source because the tumor and filament are targets needing a strong local effect, and healthy tissue and fragile glass are surroundings that a single strong approach would damage. The two also share surface features, since both direct a beam toward an internal target. About 81 percent of participants who knew the radiation problem solved the lightbulb problem, compared with about 10 percent in the control group.", "score": 6.822, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 11, "slides": [13], "q": "What is the difference between surface features and structural features?", "a": "Surface features are the specific elements used to describe a problem, such as lightbulbs, lasers, tumors and rays, and similar objects can help a person notice that two situations may be related. Structural features are the underlying principle that governs the solution. In the radiation and fragile-glass problems, a strong ray or laser would damage the surroundings on its way to an internal target, so enough intensity must reach the target while the intensity along each path stays low, and that is why several low-intensity approaches must converge. Two lightbulb versions can keep the same surface features while changing the structure.", "score": 6.444, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 11, "slides": [14, 15], "q": "How did the fragile-glass and insufficient-intensity lightbulb sources compare as sources for the radiation problem?", "a": "In the fragile-glass version, a high-intensity beam would break the glass, which matches the healthy-tissue constraint, and about 69 percent of participants who read it solved the radiation problem. In the insufficient-intensity version, the laser can only make low-intensity beams that cannot fuse the filament, so beams are combined to get enough intensity, but there is no surrounding-damage constraint. About 33 percent of participants who read that one solved the radiation problem. Both sources use converging beams, but the different transfer rates go with the difference in underlying constraints.", "score": 7.444, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 11, "slides": [16], "q": "What is analogical encoding?", "a": "Analogical encoding is comparing two problems and determining the similarities between them. Comparing source stories draws attention to relationships that appear in both, such as separate weak approaches combining at one target while constraints along the approaches prevent a single strong delivery. Having participants compare sources directs their reasoning toward the structural features, so a later target can be approached through the common relationship and does not depend entirely on the appearance of one familiar situation.", "score": 6.444, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 11, "slides": [17], "q": "What is the analogical paradox, and what is the in vivo method?", "a": "The analogical paradox is the contrast between difficulty applying analogies in laboratory tasks and routine use of analogies in real-world settings. Scientists use about 3 to 15 analogies in a one-hour laboratory meeting. In vivo problem-solving research observes people solving problems in real-world settings, which keeps the ordinary context, but it can be time-consuming and researchers cannot isolate and control variables as in a more constrained experiment.", "score": 6.444, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 11, "slides": [18, 22], "q": "What is an expert, and what do experts do better and worse?", "a": "An expert is a person recognized as extremely knowledgeable or skilled in a field after devoting a large amount of time to learning, practicing and applying it. A chess expert can have spent roughly 10,000 to 20,000 hours studying and playing. Experts solve problems in their field faster and with a higher success rate than beginners, and they spend more time analyzing a problem before proceeding. But the advantage is specific to the field, and experts are no better than novices on problems outside it.", "score": 3.844, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 11, "slides": [19], "q": "What did Chase and Simon (1973) find about chess recall?", "a": "Chase and Simon (1973) compared a chess master with a beginner who had fewer than 100 hours of chess experience. Each viewed a chessboard for five seconds and then tried to reproduce where the pieces were, using either actual game positions or random arrangements. The expert did much better for actual game positions, because long-term memory made meaningful arrangements available as chunks, groups of related information. For random arrangements those familiar chunks were not available, so the advantage reflects organized domain knowledge and not an equal advantage for every arrangement.", "who": "Chase and Simon (1973)", "score": 9.778, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 11, "slides": [20, 21], "q": "What did Chi et al. (1982) find when experts and novices sorted physics problems?", "a": "Chi et al. (1982) gave 24 physics problems to experts, who were physics professors, and novices, who were students with one semester of physics, and had them sort the problems into groups by similarity. Novices grouped by surface features, the appearance of the objects, so two problems with inclined planes were treated as alike. Experts grouped by structural features, the underlying principle. For example, the novice grouped problems 23 and 24 because both involve inclined planes, while the expert grouped problems 21 and 24 together through conservation of energy, even though 21 has a block and spring on a horizontal surface and 24 has blocks on an incline. The same problem 24 gets a different partner under the two organizations, and expertise changes which relationship is the basis for similarity, so knowledge becomes accessible through the principle that governs the solution.", "note": "The sorting study is usually cited as Chi, Feltovich and Glaser (1981), with advanced physics PhD students as the experts. Low confidence, so check before relying on it.", "who": "Chi and colleagues (1982)", "score": 9.223, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 11, "slides": [22, 24], "q": "What are the limits of expertise?", "a": "Experts spend more time analyzing a problem before proceeding, which can pay off in solving it efficiently, but the advantage is specific to the expert’s field. Expertise can make a person less open to new ways of looking at a problem, and too much knowledge can hinder creative problem solving when a familiar approach dominates. Flexible thinking sometimes means rejecting the usual procedure for a less frequently used one, since some problems require it.", "score": 4.422, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 11, "slides": [23, 24], "q": "What does creative problem solving involve beyond having an idea?", "a": "Creativity includes innovative thinking, novel ideas and new connections, and divergent thinking is open-ended and allows many potential solutions. Creative problem solving usually involves more than getting an idea, including a lengthy period of trial and error. Problem generation includes problem finding and fact finding, formulation includes defining the problem and finding ideas, solving includes evaluation and planning, and solution implementation means taking action. Ideas are necessary, since without them the process stops, but generating them does not evaluate, plan or carry out a response.", "score": 4.689, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 12, "slides": [2, 3, 4], "q": "How do decisions, judgments and reasoning relate, and what is inductive reasoning?", "a": "A decision is the process of choosing between alternatives, and reasoning is the process of drawing conclusions. Decisions are based on the judgments people make, and inductive reasoning is one of the primary mechanisms involved in making judgments. Inductive reasoning means reasoning from observations to conclusions that are probably, but not definitely, true. It is the basis of scientific investigation, where observations are made, data are collected and conclusions are drawn. The strength of an inductive argument depends on the representativeness, number and quality of the observations. Many white swans support an expectation about swan color, but one clearly observed black swan contradicts the claim that every swan is white, so a single high-quality observation can carry substantial weight. In everyday life, people use past experience to predict what will happen, especially in familiar situations, and some of this induction happens automatically.", "score": 3.631, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 12, "slides": [5], "q": "What is a heuristic, and how do the availability and representativeness heuristics differ?", "a": "Heuristics are rapid rules of thumb that use past experience to guide present behavior. They are likely to give a correct answer but are not foolproof, because they reduce the amount of information a person considers by relying on a readily available cue, which usually fits the situation but can point away from the correct judgment. The availability heuristic judges by how readily examples come to mind, and the representativeness heuristic judges by how much a case resembles a category. The difference is which property supplies the shortcut, accessibility in memory or similarity to a familiar kind of case.", "score": 4.364, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 12, "slides": [6], "q": "What is the availability heuristic?", "a": "The availability heuristic makes events that are more easily remembered seem more probable. The more examples a person can think of, the more probable the event seems. That is useful when memorable events are frequent, but memorability is also affected by distinctiveness, emotional salience and frequent encoding or retrieval, none of which make an event more common.", "score": 6.364, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 12, "slides": [7, 8], "q": "How do the r-words and causes-of-death judgments show the availability heuristic?", "a": "In the word judgment, about 70 percent of respondents say English has more words beginning with r than words with r as the third letter, but words with r third are actually three times more common. Retrieving words by first letter is easier, so those words feel more numerous. For causes of death, asthma is more likely than botulism or a tornado, yet many pick the less likely cause. A death from botulism can become front-page news while deaths from asthma go virtually unnoticed, so distinctive coverage makes the unusual cause easier to bring to mind.", "note": "The three-to-one figure is not verified. The commonly stated ratio is closer to two to one. The direction, that words with r are more common in third position, holds.", "score": 6.728, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 12, "slides": [9, 10], "q": "What is the representativeness heuristic and base-rate neglect, and how do the Robert and lawyer–engineer problems show it?", "a": "The representativeness heuristic bases a judgment on how much one event resembles another, so a person decides whether A belongs to class B from how well A’s properties match what is normally associated with B. Base-rate neglect is when that similarity displaces the background prevalence of the categories. Robert wears glasses, speaks quietly and reads a lot, and many people say he is more likely a librarian than a farmer. But America has about 2.2 million farms and 120,000 libraries, so the base rate favors farmer. In the lawyer–engineer problem a group has 70 lawyers and 30 engineers. With no other information, the chance of an engineer is 30 out of 100 and people use that correctly. When Jack is described as conservative, careful, ambitious and enjoying home carpentry, sailing and mathematical puzzles, people tend to call him an engineer. So base rates are used when they are all that is available and disregarded once descriptive information is given.", "note": "The 2.2 million and 120,000 are counts of farms and libraries, not of people in each occupation. Farmers still outnumber librarians, so the conclusion stands, but the comparison is loose.", "score": 6.527, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 12, "slides": [11], "q": "What is the conjunction rule, and why is the Linda problem an error?", "a": "The conjunction rule states that the probability of two events occurring together cannot be greater than the probability of either event alone, since every case satisfying both conditions also satisfies each one. Linda is described as single, outspoken, bright, trained in philosophy, concerned with discrimination and social justice, and involved in antinuclear demonstrations. About 82 percent choose Linda is a bank teller and active in the feminist movement over Linda is a bank teller. The extra detail makes the description fit the person they were told about, but feminist bank tellers are a subset of bank tellers, so being in the smaller set automatically puts her in the larger one. The error is picking the better-fitting story over the nested-set probability relationship, which is a representativeness effect.", "score": 7.636, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 12, "slides": [12, 13, 14], "q": "In the hospital problem which hospital has more days with over 60 percent boys, and what does the law of large numbers say?", "a": "The larger hospital has about 45 births a day and the smaller about 15, and about half of babies are boys. The question is which hospital records more days when over 60 percent are boys, and the correct answer is the smaller hospital. The most common response was about the same, chosen by 56 percent, which ignores how many births are behind each day’s percentage. Smaller samples vary more, so an extreme proportion is more likely in the group of 15 births, while the larger hospital’s daily proportion is more stable. Choosing the larger hospital confuses the absolute number of boys with how often an extreme proportion occurs. The law of large numbers says that as more individuals are randomly drawn, the group becomes more representative of the population. People often expect small samples to be representative too, which understates how much a small sample can vary.", "score": 6.831, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 12, "slides": [15], "q": "What is an illusory correlation, and how does it connect to stereotypes and availability?", "a": "An illusory correlation is a relationship that appears to exist even though it does not exist or is much weaker than assumed. Stereotypes are oversimplified generalizations about a group, often emphasizing negative characteristics, and they direct attention to behaviors that fit the expected description. Selective attention and remembering the matching instances make the stereotype feel supported, so the apparent evidence is shaped by what was noticed and retained. The link to availability is that stereotype-consistent behaviors are easier to bring to mind because they got particular attention, and their availability is then mistaken for evidence that the behavior is strongly associated with the group.", "score": 4.364, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 12, "slides": [16, 17], "q": "What is myside bias and confirmation bias? What did Lord and colleagues (1979) find, and how does the 2, 4, 6 task show confirmation bias?", "a": "Confirmation bias is the tendency to seek information that fits a hypothesis and overlook information that argues against it, and myside bias is one form of it, generating and evaluating evidence and testing hypotheses in ways biased toward one’s own opinions and attitudes. Lord and colleagues (1979) had people who favored capital punishment and people who opposed it read the same article. Those in favor interpreted it as favorable to their position and those against interpreted it as favorable to theirs, focusing on agreeing information and disregarding the rest. In the 2, 4, 6 task, participants get 2, 4, 6 as an example of a hidden rule, write their own sets of three numbers, get feedback only on whether each fits the rule, and then propose the rule. The most common hypothesis is increasing intervals of two, and testing only sequences that fit it gives repeated yes feedback. But the actual rule is any three numbers in increasing order, so only trying to disconfirm, testing the boundaries, would reveal the narrow belief is wrong.", "note": "In the original study participants read two mixed-evidence studies on capital punishment, not the same article, and the result is usually described as biased assimilation and attitude polarization.", "who": "Lord and colleagues (1979)", "score": 9.055, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 12, "slides": [18, 19], "q": "What is expected utility theory, and why does it not fully describe what people choose?", "a": "Utility refers to how desirable an outcome is because it serves the person’s interests. Expected utility theory assumes people are rational and, with all relevant information, choose the alternative with maximum expected utility, combining the value of possible outcomes with their chances, and the monetary version uses the maximum expected monetary payoff. It gives specific procedures for determining the best choice. But people find value in things other than money, and many decisions do not maximize the probability of the best outcome, so people are not utility-maximization machines. Casino gambling is an example, because people choose it despite knowing the odds are strongly against them.", "note": "Casino gambling shows that people do not maximize expected money. It does not by itself refute expected utility theory, because utility can include enjoyment of gambling.", "score": 3.837, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 12, "slides": [20], "q": "What happens in the jellybean problem, and what does it show?", "a": "Participants earn one dollar whenever they draw a red jellybean, up to seven dollars, and choose between two bowls. One bowl has 1 red bean out of 10, a 10 percent chance, and the other has 7 red beans out of 100, a 7 percent chance. Most participants choose the 7-out-of-100 bowl. The larger number of red beans is attractive even though the probability of drawing one is worse. A larger number of favorable items draws attention away from the denominator, the total number of possible draws, so the decision depends on considerations other than the actual probabilities of winning.", "note": "The slide says most participants choose the 7-out-of-100 bowl. I could not verify that a majority chose it in the original finding.", "score": 7.545, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 12, "slides": [21], "q": "What happened to travel after September 11, and what does it show about risk decisions?", "a": "After September 11, air travel decreased and driving increased. The probability of dying in a car accident is much greater than while flying, and the reported number of Americans who died on the road while avoiding flying exceeded the total number of passengers killed on the four hijacked flights. Avoiding the feared event increased exposure to a more likely danger, because the emotional impact of the attacks is a different basis for a decision than the relative probabilities.", "score": 1.364, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 12, "slides": [22], "q": "What is the convoy dilemma, and what did Ritov and Baron (1999) find?", "a": "A convoy of food trucks is traveling to a refugee camp during a famine, and rerouting saves 1,000 people at a second camp but 100 people in the first camp die. Rerouting has the better net count, 1,000 − 100 = 900 versus 100 − 1,000 = −900. Ritov and Baron (1999) found that 63 percent did not choose to reroute, and some would keep the original route even if only one person at the first camp would die. Reluctance to cause a loss can outweigh the larger number saved elsewhere.", "who": "Ritov and Baron (1999)", "score": 10.727, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 12, "slides": [23, 24], "q": "What is the Sacred Value Protection Model (Tetlock, Kristel, Elson, Green and Lerner, 2000), and what are taboo and tragic tradeoffs?", "a": "The Sacred Value Protection Model of Tetlock, Kristel, Elson, Green and Lerner (2000) proposes that people want to protect what they hold sacred, and that sacred values are compartmentalized separately from secular values. A taboo tradeoff intermingles the sacred and secular domains, so treating a sacred concern as exchangeable for a secular benefit feels threatening and provokes moral outrage. A tragic tradeoff compares two sacred values with each other, and people are less morally outraged by it. In the taboo scenario, Robert can save Johnny, a five-year-old needing a liver transplant that costs the hospital one million dollars, or spend the money on equipment and salaries to recruit talented doctors. In the tragic scenario, two children need a liver but only one is available.", "who": "Tetlock, Kristel, Elson, Green and Lerner (2000)", "score": 9.236, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 12, "slides": [25], "q": "What did Tetlock and colleagues (2000) find about moral outrage in the Johnny scenarios?", "a": "In Tetlock and colleagues (2000), moral outrage was rated for taboo and tragic tradeoffs, with separate outcomes for saving Johnny and not saving him. Under the taboo tradeoff, not saving Johnny produced much greater outrage than saving him, and choosing other uses for the money instead of the child’s life received the strongest negative response. Under the tragic tradeoff, outrage was similar whether Johnny was saved or not, because choosing either child leaves another sacred concern unmet. So there is an interaction between the type of value conflict and the decision made within it.", "who": "Tetlock and colleagues (2000)", "score": 9.727, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 12, "slides": [26, 27, 28, 29], "q": "What did Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007) study, and how did money and symbolic offers affect moral absolutists and non-absolutists?", "a": "Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007) studied sacred-value tradeoffs in an ongoing political conflict, with 601 Israeli settlers, 535 Palestinian refugees and 719 Palestinian students. They offered three deals. The taboo deal asked Palestinians to recognize the sacred and historic right of the Jewish people to Israel, with two states and a Palestinian state in 99 percent of the West Bank and Gaza. Taboo+ added a material incentive, one billion dollars a year for ten years paid by Israel. The symbolic version added Israeli recognition of the historic and legitimate right of Palestinians to their own state and an apology for wrongs done. Moral absolutists treat the issue as a sacred value and reject compromise regardless of benefit, about 46 percent of settlers for never giving up part of the Land of Israel and about 54 percent of the students for the right of return and Jerusalem. Non-absolutists also have strong preferences against compromise but do not treat the issues as sacred. For absolutists, adding money increased anger, disgust and support for violence, while for non-absolutists it reduced them. Symbolic recognition was associated with lower anger or disgust and lower support for violence, so material benefit and symbolic reciprocity are different.", "who": "Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007)", "score": 8.463, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 12, "slides": [30, 31], "q": "What did Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010) study, and how were sacred values measured?", "a": "Dehghani, Atran and colleagues (2010) and Dehghani, Iliev and colleagues (2009) studied sacred values in relation to Iran’s nuclear program, asking whether people who hold the program sacred would accept material benefits or costs in exchange for compromise. An incentive offers a benefit for agreement, and a sanction is a disincentive that imposes a cost for refusing. The online study had 1,997 Iranian participants, in Farsi, and measured approval, anger, and whether a proposal seemed misleading. Using a measure based on Baron and Spranca (1997), the sacred-value response is that giving up the program should not be done no matter how great the consequences, and about 14 percent for nuclear energy and 13 percent for nuclear weapons chose it.", "who": "Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010)", "score": 9.8, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 12, "slides": [32, 33], "q": "What were the deals in the study by Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010), and what happened for participants with a sacred value?", "a": "In the study by Dehghani, Atran and colleagues (2010) and Dehghani, Iliev and colleagues (2009), the design was 2 × 3, energy or weapons crossed with three deals. Taboo had Iran give up the program and its facilities to the United Nations in return for Israel doing the same, Taboo+ added 40 billion dollars from the United Nations to Iran, and Taboo− added the threat of additional sanctions if Iran refused. Participants rated their own approval, whether people would be angry if the government approved, and whether the deal seemed misleading. For energy, participants with a sacred value showed lower approval with the financial incentive and with sanctions than with the baseline, and anticipated anger was highest with the incentive and elevated with sanctions. Participants without a sacred value had much higher approval and lower anger, with little variation across conditions. So for a sacred commitment, improving the material balance does not necessarily improve acceptability.", "who": "Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010)", "score": 7.964, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 13, "slides": [2, 3], "q": "How do emotions shape decisions, and what are expected emotions?", "a": "Emotions take part in decision making and do not only get in its way. People with damage to an area of their prefrontal cortex who have flattened emotions have impaired decision making. Anxious people tend toward risk avoidance, passing up choices that could lead to large negative consequences, and optimistic people ignore negative information and focus on positive information, which in excess can lead to poor decisions. Expected emotions are the emotions people predict they will feel about an outcome, and they are one determinant of risk aversion. People inaccurately predict their emotions, believing a particular loss will have a greater impact than a gain of the same size.", "score": 4.506, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 13, "slides": [4], "q": "What did Kermer and colleagues (2006) find when they compared expected and actual emotions?", "a": "Kermer and colleagues (2006) gave participants $5 and told them a coin flip would win them another $5 or lose them $3, after rating their baseline happiness. Beforehand they expected losing $3 to lower happiness by about 4 points and winning $5 to raise it by about 2, so the expected loss was about twice the expected gain. Afterward the actual drop from losing was only about 1 point and the rise from winning about 1.2. People overestimated the negative feeling because they do not consider the coping mechanisms that get activated and soften a loss.", "who": "Kermer and colleagues (2006)", "score": 9.533, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 13, "slides": [5], "q": "What are incidental emotions, and what did Simonsohn (2007, 2009) find about the weather?", "a": "Incidental emotions are emotions not specifically related to the decision, such as those from a person’s general disposition, a recent experience or the surroundings, like the weather. Simonsohn (2007) analyzed university admissions decisions and found applicants’ academic attributes were weighted more heavily on cloudy days than sunny days. Simonsohn (2009) found that prospective students visiting a highly rated university were more likely to enroll if they had visited on a cloudy day. The weather is unrelated to the applicants or the school but still changed the decision.", "who": "Simonsohn (2007, 2009)", "score": 9.533, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 13, "slides": [6], "q": "What did Lerner and colleagues (2004) find about mood and buying and selling prices?", "a": "In Lerner and colleagues (2004), participants watched a sad clip of a person dying, a disgust clip of a person using a dirty toilet, or a neutral clip of fish at the Great Barrier Reef, and then set a selling price and a buying price for highlighters. For selling, neutral was about $4.50, disgust about $2.70 and sad about $3.00. For buying, neutral was about $3.50, disgust about $3.00 and sad about $4.50. Disgust lowered both prices, while sadness lowered the selling price and raised the buying price. The clips were unrelated to the choice, so these were incidental emotions, and they changed how much the same object seemed worth.", "who": "Lerner and colleagues (2004)", "score": 9.933, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 13, "slides": [7, 8], "q": "How does the context of a decision and the way choices are presented matter, using the cesarean case and the opt-in versus opt-out donor rates?", "a": "Decisions depend on context. Physicians saw the same hypothetical cesarean test case in three contexts, alone first as the control, after four other cases with serious complications, or after four fairly routine cases. Significantly more doctors recommended cesarean after reading the non-serious cases first, so the cases just considered changed how the same patient was judged. Decisions also depend on how choices are presented. In opt-in countries a person must take an active step to be an organ donor, and consent rates are below 28 percent, while in opt-out countries a person is a donor unless they request otherwise, and consent rates are over 99 percent. The status quo bias is the tendency to do nothing when faced with a difficult decision, and since doing nothing leaves a person a non-donor under opt-in and a donor under opt-out, the rates end up far apart. The story The International Community shows a choice of the same kind, where the birds can only pick the sauce they are eaten with, not whether they are eaten.", "note": "Not every opt-out country exceeds 99 percent consent. Reported effective consent in opt-out countries ranges from about 86 percent to about 99.98 percent. The usual definition of status quo bias is a preference for the current state or default, and doing nothing is the mechanism, not the definition.", "score": 7.68, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 13, "slides": [9, 11, 12], "q": "What is the framing effect, and what did Tversky and Kahneman (1981) find in the disease problem?", "a": "The framing effect is when decisions are influenced by how a decision is stated, even when the facts are the same. Tversky and Kahneman (1981) found that gain framing leads to risk aversion, preferring the sure option, and loss framing leads to risk taking, preferring the gamble. In the disease problem, 600 people are expected to die. In the gain frame, Program A saves 200 for certain and Program B has a 1/3 chance that 600 are saved and a 2/3 chance that none are saved, and 72 percent chose A and 28 percent chose B. In the loss frame, Program C means 400 die for certain and Program D has a 1/3 chance that no one dies and a 2/3 chance that 600 die, and 78 percent chose D and 22 percent chose C. Program C is the same outcome as A, since saving 200 of 600 means 400 die, and D is the same gamble as B, so only the wording changed and the majority switched from the sure option to the gamble. A risk-aversion strategy avoids an uncertain outcome in favor of a certain one, and a risk-taking strategy accepts uncertainty in the hope of avoiding a certain loss.", "who": "Tversky and Kahneman (1981)", "score": 9.231, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 13, "slides": [13, 14], "q": "What is neuroeconomics, and how does the ultimatum game used by Sanfey and colleagues (2003) work? What is the rational decision for the responder?", "a": "Neuroeconomics combines psychology, neuroscience and economics. Psychology contributes how people think and decide, neuroscience contributes what happens in the brain, and economics contributes how choices about money and value are analyzed. One finding is that decisions are influenced by emotions that are associated with activity in specific brain areas. In the ultimatum game used by Sanfey and colleagues (2003), a proposer gets $10 and offers the responder a split. The responder accepts, and the money is divided as offered, or rejects, and neither player receives anything. According to utility theory, the rational decision for the responder is to accept any offer, because even $1 is worth more than the nothing that comes from rejecting.", "who": "Sanfey and colleagues (2003)", "score": 6.48, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 13, "slides": [15], "q": "What did Sanfey and colleagues (2003) find about unfair offers, human versus computer partners, and the anterior insula?", "a": "In Sanfey and colleagues (2003), participants played 20 games, 10 with human partners and 10 with computer partners. Offers of $5 and $5 and of $7 and $3 were accepted almost every time, about 100 percent and about 95 percent for both partner types. Lower offers separated the partners. An $8 and $2 offer was accepted about 53 percent of the time from a human and about 85 percent from a computer, and a $9 and $1 offer was accepted about 37 percent from a human and about 68 percent from a computer. Participants often rejected low offers because they became angry that the offers were unfair, and they were less angry with an unfair computer. Participants with more activation of the right anterior insula, an area connected with emotional states, rejected more offers, even though rejecting meant getting nothing. So emotion is important in decision making, and it can lead people to give up money.", "who": "Sanfey and colleagues (2003)", "score": 8.6, "tier": "high"}, {"module": 13, "slides": [16, 17, 18], "q": "What are deductive reasoning, a syllogism and validity, and how is validity different from truth? What is belief bias?", "a": "Deductive reasoning is determining whether a conclusion logically follows from premises, which are the statements the reasoning starts from. A syllogism, introduced by Aristotle, has two premises and a conclusion, and a categorical syllogism describes the relation between two categories using all, no or some, such as All birds are animals, All animals eat food, therefore all birds eat food. A syllogism is valid if its conclusion follows logically from its premises, and if the premises of a valid syllogism are true then the conclusion must be true. Validity depends on the form of the reasoning while truth depends on whether the statements match the world. All birds are animals, all animals have four legs, therefore all birds have four legs is valid because it has the same form, but the conclusion is false only because the second premise is false. Syllogisms can also be invalid even though every statement seems reasonable, such as All of the students are tired, some tired people are irritable, some of the students are irritable. Belief bias is the tendency to think a syllogism is valid if its conclusion is believable, and 71 percent of participants judged invalid syllogisms with believable conclusions as valid.", "score": 7.009, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 13, "slides": [19, 20], "q": "What is the answer to the pool table problem, and what is the mental model approach (Johnson-Laird, 1999)?", "a": "There is a black ball directly above the cue ball, the green ball is to the right of the cue ball, and a red ball is between them. The speaker moves so the red ball is between the speaker and the black ball, and the cue ball is to the left of the line of sight. Most people solve it by imagining how the balls are arranged on the table, which is the basis of Johnson-Laird’s (1999) proposal that people use mental models for deductive reasoning. A mental model is a specific situation represented in a person’s mind to help determine the validity of syllogisms. The person first creates a model of the situation described by the premises and generates tentative conclusions, then looks for exceptions to falsify the model, a case where the premises are true but the conclusion is not, and finally determines validity from whether an exception was found. A conclusion is valid only if it cannot be refuted by any model of the premises, so if even one model fits the premises but not the conclusion, the conclusion does not follow.", "who": "Johnson-Laird (1999)", "score": 6.52, "tier": "low"}, {"module": 13, "slides": [21, 22, 23], "q": "What are the four conditional syllogisms, and why are modus ponens and modus tollens valid?", "a": "Conditional syllogisms have a first premise of the form If p, then q, and p is the condition and q is what follows. The four types differ in the second premise. Syllogism 1 goes from p to therefore q, syllogism 2 from not q to therefore not p, syllogism 3 from q to therefore p, and syllogism 4 from not p to therefore not q. Two are valid and two are not. Syllogism 1 is modus ponens. If I study I’ll get a good grade, I studied, therefore I’ll get a good grade. Since the condition is met, what follows from it has to follow too, and 97 percent of participants judged it correctly. Syllogism 2 is modus tollens. If I study I’ll get a good grade, I didn’t get a good grade, therefore I didn’t study. If studying always leads to a good grade, missing the grade means the studying did not happen. It is just as valid as modus ponens, yet only 60 percent judged it correctly.", "score": 7.04, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 13, "slides": [24, 25], "q": "Why are conditional syllogisms 3 and 4 not valid, and how often did people judge them correctly?", "a": "Syllogism 3 is If I study I’ll get a good grade, I got a good grade, therefore I studied. It is not valid because the first premise says what happens when someone studies, but it does not say studying is the only way to get a good grade. The same form with different content is If I live in Tucson then I live in Arizona, I live in Arizona, therefore I live in Tucson, where it is easier to see that a person can live in Arizona without living in Tucson. Syllogism 4 is If I study I’ll get a good grade, I didn’t study, therefore I didn’t get a good grade. It is not valid because the first premise says nothing about what happens when someone does not study, so a person could get a good grade for another reason. Only 40 percent judged each of these correctly, compared with 97 percent and 60 percent for the two valid types, so both valid types were judged correctly more often than both invalid types.", "score": 7.56, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 13, "slides": [26, 27], "q": "What is the Wason four-card problem, which cards should you turn over, and what is the falsification principle?", "a": "In the Wason four-card problem, four cards read E, K, 4 and 7, and the task is to find the minimum number of cards to turn over to test the rule If there is a vowel on one side, then there is an even number on the other side. The answer is to turn over the E and the 7. A 7 with a vowel on its other side would break the rule, so turning it over is a real test, and a 4 cannot break the rule whatever is on its back. 46 percent of participants chose the 4, which is an error, and only 7 percent chose the 7. The falsification principle states that to test a rule you must look for situations that falsify it, meaning prove it false, and most participants fail to do this. When the problem is stated concretely, with the rule If drinking beer, then 21 years old and cards reading Beer, Soda, 16 years old and 21 years old, 73 percent answered correctly. The cards to turn over are beer and 16 years old, because someone drinking beer while under 21 is the only case that breaks the rule.", "note": "Wason’s percentages are for combinations of cards. About 46 percent chose E and 4 and about 7 percent chose E and 7, so only 7 percent chose the 7 understates how many selected it.", "score": 7.773, "tier": "mid"}, {"module": 13, "slides": [28], "q": "What is a permission schema, and why does it make the card problem easier?", "a": "A permission schema is the idea that if A is satisfied, B can be carried out. A schema is a familiar pattern of knowledge, and a permission schema is the pattern of a rule that grants permission once a condition is met, such as a drinking age. It is used in the concrete versions because people are familiar with rules of this kind, so a permission rule is easier to reason about than an abstract rule about letters and numbers. In the beer version, being 21 is the condition that must be satisfied and drinking beer is what can then be carried out. Context is important, because the same logical problem becomes easier in a familiar setting that calls up a permission schema. Familiarity is not always important, so the improvement does not come from familiarity alone.", "score": 6.4, "tier": "low"}];
